{"step_index":2064,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:50:19Z","input_tokens":2643,"cache_read_tokens":71280,"output_tokens":2031,"thinking":"Analyzing Phase 4's search and filter requirements, focusing on the distinct filter categories needed. These filters encompass topic, difficulty, status (including the 'Review' state), algorithmic patterns, and bookmarked status. Each filter category must include all specified options.\n\n\nImplementing a \"Review\" status necessitates adding a new `review` array within the progress tracking, along with associated methods to manage this status.  The problem status function requires updating to incorporate \"review\" as a possible state, overriding the previous \"solved\" state. The filter options, problem row and modal, and stats calculation also need updating to incorporate and correctly display the review status.\nA quick bookmark filter toggle will allow filtering on bookmarked items in addition to the status filter. Title, difficulty, and learning order must be available as sort options.\n\n\nAdding sorting options necessitates integrating the specified sort criteria within the data retrieval and display logic, along with updating the UI to include a sort dropdown. The pagination requirements involve implementing page size selection, complete pagination controls, and page indicator display.\n\nEach problem now requires a unique route or a dedicated detail view. This requires updating the routing system to support individual problem URLs and a corresponding component to render detailed problem information.\n\n\nThe routing system should incorporate URL hash changes to enable deep linking to individual problems, ensuring the appropriate DSA view and problem detail view are activated based on the hash. Implementing a dedicated detail view alongside the modal provides shareable links and enhances the user experience, while also ensuring proper history tracking. A function that handles opening a problem detail based on the ID is necessary.\n\nTo prevent duplicate records, an `importProblems` function will be implemented, which will allow for importing a new list of problems with options to handle such scenarios. The goal is to avoid redundancy during this data import process.\n\n\nA new problem import function will be created, accessible through both `DsaDashboard` and `window.DSA_PROBLEMS_LIBRARY`. Duplicate detection must check for existing IDs, LeetCode numbers, normalized titles, and source URLs.\n\nDuplicate handling offers options to either skip the problem or update the existing entry while preserving user-specific data; an import summary report will provide statistics and identify duplicates. The UI will incorporate an import interface, offering a preview of the imported data with duplicate detection details.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python -c \"\nwith open('app_build/styles.css', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    c = f.read()\nfor term in ['pagination-bar', 'problem-toolbar', 'filter-pill', 'filter-select', 'table-meta-bar']:\n    print(f'{term}: {\\\"FOUND\\\" if term in c else \\\"NOT FOUND\\\"}')\n\"","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer","IsDaemon":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking CSS classes","toolSummary":"Check styles.css"}}]}
