{"step_index":2076,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:39Z","input_tokens":2588,"cache_read_tokens":75847,"output_tokens":4536,"thinking":"Reviewing the specified features for Phase 4 reveals the need to implement filtering by topic, difficulty, status (including a 'Review' option), algorithmic pattern, and bookmark status. Sorting functionality by title (A-Z, Z-A) and difficulty (Easy -> Hard, Hard -> Easy) also needs to be added.\n\n\nImplementing a \"Learning Order\" sort will require mapping problem IDs to their intended sequence. Pagination will improve the user experience with selectable page sizes and navigation controls. Unique routes and a detail view (modal or drawer) with URL handling are necessary for direct navigation and shareable links.\n\nDuplicate record prevention is crucial during import; this involves an import engine with deduplication based on several identifiers. A skip or update strategy, along with a verification report, is planned. Finally, the problem status tracking needs enhancement with 'Review' and 'Bookmarked' options and related methods for managing these states within the progress data.\n\n\nThe existing `getProblemStatus` function needs modification to accurately reflect a problem's state, specifically handling cases where a problem is marked as both solved and for review simultaneously. This necessitates adjusting how the `review` status is managed. The `review` state will hold IDs independently, allowing for a dedicated \"Review\" indicator in the problem row. The filtering logic in `getFilteredProblems()` must accommodate the new 'review' status, along with 'bookmarked' and 'with_notes', while considering that 'attempted' excludes solved problems. The filtering criteria needs expansion to include the 'review' status, as described in the requirements.\n\n\nImplemented status filtering with pills for \"All\", \"Not Started\", \"Attempted\", \"Solved\", and \"Review,\" alongside a dedicated \"Bookmarked\" toggle. Developed sorting options for learning order (ascending/descending), title (ascending/descending), difficulty (ascending/descending), and LeetCode number (ascending/descending) within the filtering mechanism. The comparison logic needs review.\n\n\nSorting implementation appears functional; each case correctly sorts the list based on the selected criteria: title, difficulty, or LeetCode number. The default sort, implicitly, preserves the original learning order.\n\nConsidering pagination, the default page size of 25 needs to be configurable, allowing options for 10, 50, 100, and \"all.\" The UI will require a selector to set the page size and navigation controls, including first/last page buttons. Distinct routes, for detail views, will need to be configured.\n\n\nImplementing unique URL routes will require modifying hashchange event handling to parse problem IDs and trigger modal/detail view updates. A copy-to-clipboard button and route breadcrumb need addition within the modal or detail view. Support for a dedicated detail view requires augmenting the sub-tab switching logic.\n\nA deduplication strategy must be devised for import to prevent duplicate records. This will need a method to identify existing records and offer a choice of skipping or updating existing problems. Verification is needed to confirm the successful import.\n\n\nImplemented a method for importing problems with deduplication using a variety of keys. Duplicate detection relies on existing IDs, LeetCode numbers, normalized titles, and normalized URLs for efficient lookups.  A report is generated to summarize import status, including skipped or updated records and errors. The method handles invalid problem objects by reporting errors.\n\n\nDeduplication logic is complete and uses several keys for identifying existing records: problem ID, LeetCode number, normalized title, and normalized URL. Duplicate detection will check each key in the stated order. Duplicate records are either updated or skipped, based on the `onDuplicate` option.\nA report is generated that lists all skipped or updated duplicates and any encountered errors during the import process. If a problem record lacks an ID, a valid unique ID will be assigned.\n\n\nIf a problem ID is missing or already exists, a new, unique ID must be generated.  A `validRecord` object must be created, using either provided data or default values for attributes like title, URL, difficulty, topics, description, and complexity. Default values ensure data integrity.\n\n\nA button for importing problems from a JSON source will be added to the UI toolbar, which will trigger an import modal.  This import function will report the number of imported records, skipped duplicates, and total catalog size. A review button will be added to each problem row. It toggles the review status on the corresponding problem.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\dsa_dashboard.js","EndLine":1715,"StartLine":1640,"toolAction":"Viewing renderProblemExplorer toolbar","toolSummary":"View problem explorer toolbar"}}]}
